Z||000003||batboy||12-18-1999||11:50 PM||||This longitudinally mounted V8 engine is a tempting swap. You do lose your trunk of course. The transaxles were designed for V8 engines. I have a 87 SE with a bad motor. I'll probably swap a 4.3 in instead, because I can get the engine from my Dad, so it'll be cheaper. Still, mighty tempting, except I'd use a Pontiac GTO 400 Ram Air IV engine with a tri-power intake and 3 deuces. SWEET!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-19-1999||12:33 AM||||batty--Can we say, "More Power"?! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">:||||reg||1
Z||000005||olympic||12-19-1999||09:31 PM||||This swap would be great if you were building a car that would strictly be used in the quarter mile. It would have tons of power and transaxle durability to go along with it.  I just wouldn't want to take this car around an on ramp or any kind of race track. I've seen the olds/caddy FWD setup and the centerline of the engine is BEHIND the axles. So you'd have a 600 pound engine and a huge transaxle hanging out behind your Fiero taking the weight off the front wheels. Good for traction but terrible for handling.<BR>     I've always thought a neat project would be to take a Chevy S10 and put one 501 caddy and transaxle in the front and another in the rear. How does 1000 cubic inches, 800 HP and 1100 lbs of torque(2 bone stock 1970 501's) in a vehicle weighing less than 4000 lbs sound???? And I bet it could be done for less than the cost on a new Geo Metro.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cobra427||12-19-1999||09:58 PM||||Does anyone care to see a front-engined Fiero project? If so,go to: <A HREF="http://www.boomtastic.com/prostreet.htm" TARGET=_blank>www.boomtastic.com/prostreet.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||12-20-1999||02:09 PM||||olympic, I remember seeing an article in one of the hot rod magazines a LONG time ago. Someone did a similar setup except the engines were built up in a 60's muscle car (442 maybe?). When the article was written it looked great but they still hadn't been able to make it control it at anything approaching full throttle. Something about getting the engines and power to the ground exactly in sync. Seems wierd things happen to steering when one set of wheels is going faster than the other.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-20-1999||02:41 PM||||Another was done by Car & Driver (or R & T?).  They did a CRX <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.  They called it the CRX2 (that's supposed to be "squared").  Seems like they spent tons of time and $$ on it and never did get both ends to work together right.  And they had them connected to each other somehow.||||reg||1
Z||000009||olympic||12-21-1999||01:24 PM||||I think that if both trannys were automatics there wouldn't be many problems. Any slight variations in the RPM between the engines would be absorbed by the trannys. It has to be possible because there is a company in Florida who installs 2 Northstars in an Eldorado. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||M King||12-21-1999||03:59 PM||||I'd like to see 2 northstars in a fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000011||Monkeyman||12-21-1999||10:36 PM||||I saw a pic of the Eldorado with the extra engine in the back.  They moved the rear wheels back to the end of the car.  Very ugly!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||DRH||12-22-1999||02:08 PM||||I didn't mean to say it's impossible. The best I remember, the one I saw did fine cruising around it was on the dragstrip it had problems. I think the problem was more related to one axle spinning the tires and the other one hooking up. The one I saw was in the late 70's or early 80's. I'm sure modern electronics (which the Northstar is famous for) would make syncronizing a lot more precise.<P>Two Northstars in a Fiero would be great even if you couldn't "put the pedal to the metal"!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 12-22-1999).]||||reg||1
