Z||000014||loafer87gt||12-22-1999||04:17 PM||||Mu uncle is always talking abot an old Studabaker that his buddy had that had two engines in it. He claims that when his buddy would rev the engine the frame would twist so much that the wheels on the opposite corners of the front and back would lift off the ground. Sounds like one heck of a story or an impressive car.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||12-22-1999||06:23 PM||||It would make sense if the engines were opposed.  You know, one facing the front and one facing the back.  It doesn't seem that they could work together that way though.||||reg||1
Z||000016||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||07:37 PM||||You are inventing a completely different beast.  I have seen cars where one engine handles motion in one direction and another handles the opposite direction.  CAN YOU IMAGINE HOW EASY OUR TRANSMISSIONS WOULD BE W/O REVERSE!?!  For anyone taking this literally yes that was intended to be a joke.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000017||DJRice||12-22-1999||11:52 PM||||Actually, if the engines were opposed, it would be ideal to interface to some kind of transmission.  Visualize one engine turning a gear clockwise.  Now visualize an indentical engine mounted oppositely such that the flywheels were side by side. turning another gear. In relation to the engine, both primary gears are turning clockwise.  However, when one is oppositely mounted, if you pick a reference point between the engines, one primary gear is rotating clockwise and one is rotating counter clockwise.  If a secondary gear were meshed between the two, it would benefit from the torque of both motors.  Obviously, maintaining an even throttle level between the two engines would be necessary, but that's a whole other long-winded post!<P>Is that understandable?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Phil||12-23-1999||06:25 AM||||Many moons ago Micky Thompson did that very thing on one of his salt flat cars. He meshed the flywheels together.||||reg||1
